00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.566
On Facebook,
you can log in using your password, 

00:00:57.566 --> 00:01:02.000
but you can also link your account with Gmail
to log in with your Gmail account.

00:01:03.233 --> 00:01:04.550
The protocol to do this

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:07.700
is Oauth
and it is widely used across the web.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:11.600
Very simplified flow of this process
looks like this.

00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:15.266
You are on Facebook
and you click log in with Gmail button.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.950
Facebook generates some parameters for you
and redirects you to Gmail.

00:01:20.416 --> 00:01:22.550
Then in Gmail, you have to log in.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.216
But usually you are logged in already.

00:01:25.433 --> 00:01:27.316
So you only choose the account

00:01:27.316 --> 00:01:31.016
and if you only have one account,
Gmail will just flash in front of you

00:01:31.016 --> 00:01:33.533
and you will be redirected back
to Facebook.

00:01:35.066 --> 00:01:36.866
The parameters, among others,

00:01:36.866 --> 00:01:39.983
will contain the code.

00:01:39.983 --> 00:01:42.000
Importantly, up until now,

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.616
Facebook and Gmail servers
did not communicate with each other.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.966
Only now, when Facebook receives
the code, sends the request

00:01:51.416 --> 00:01:56.000
to Gmail server and gives you back the
session token if it's okay.

00:01:57.533 --> 00:01:58.466
So this code

00:01:58.466 --> 00:02:03.300
is very valuable because it allows you
to exchange it for the session cookie.

00:02:03.683 --> 00:02:08.150
So even the attacker would see it,
they could also take over your account.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:13.216
And across the years, we saw many attacks
that looked more or less like this.

00:02:13.583 --> 00:02:17.400
The attacker has to trick the victim
into visiting their website.

00:02:17.783 --> 00:02:21.766
The attacker's website sends the victim
into the authentication flow.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.400
But before the original website consumes
the code,

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:30.350
the attacker takes it over
and hijacks user's account.

00:02:31.916 --> 00:02:35.183
And this is exactly what we'll try to do
today.

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:38.300
However, in modern web

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.783
and on a hardened target like Facebook,
it is not easy.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.265
Definitely much harder
than in the early days of Oauth.

00:02:46.817 --> 00:02:50.183
In particular, there are two problems
that we need to solve.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:54.050
The first one is that the code is one time
use only.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:59.300
It means if the victim consumes
the code - sends this code to Facebook,

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:03.383
the attacker can't do anything
even if they have the code.

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:05.816
And the other is,

00:03:05.816 --> 00:03:08.100
of course, leaking the code somehow.

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:12.916
So to prevent the code
from being used by the victim,

00:03:13.066 --> 00:03:17.777
we need to stop this flow somewhere
between Gmail and Facebook.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:22.583
On Facebook,
If you make too many requests,

00:03:22.583 --> 00:03:26.483
your account will enter a state
where you need to fill in the CAPTCHA.

00:03:26.566 --> 00:03:29.900
I would call it the captcha state.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:33.316
Whatever you will try to do on Facebook,
you will be shown this window.

00:03:34.583 --> 00:03:37.700
And it is a very good candidate
to solve the first problem

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:42.433
because if the victim's account will be in
the lockout state, in the captcha state,

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:47.516
after they finish the authentication flow,
they won't use the code.

00:03:47.516 --> 00:03:51.566
And importantly, the code will be
in the URL of the website,

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.933
giving us some chance to leak it.

00:03:55.516 --> 00:03:56.550
But how do

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.550
we force the victim's account
into the CAPTCHA state?

00:04:00.466 --> 00:04:02.566
The answer to that is that we don't.

00:04:02.933 --> 00:04:07.916
Instead, we will introduce
the CAPTCHA state into our own account.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:13.133
And then we'll use two bugs
to log out the victim from their account

00:04:13.133 --> 00:04:19.683
using one CSRF and then log in the victim
into our account using another CSRF.

00:04:19.683 --> 00:04:25.286
These two bugs, even though they are very low
risk by themselves, chained together here

00:04:25.286 --> 00:04:27.716
will allow us to move

00:04:27.716 --> 00:04:30.316
the victim's Facebook
into the captcha state.

00:04:31.666 --> 00:04:33.883
Now, you might be asking yourself,

00:04:33.883 --> 00:04:38.116
how do we take over a victim's account
if they are logged in to our account?

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.100
But the thing is that
in case of this attack,

00:04:41.566 --> 00:04:44.600
Facebook
won't actually take part in this process.

00:04:45.233 --> 00:04:47.900
We are interested in Gmail
generating the code

00:04:48.016 --> 00:04:51.950
and taking over this code, which
will allow us to take over the account.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:57.716
So it doesn't matter what account is
the victim logged in on Facebook.com.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.683
So we are able to log out the victim
from their account,

00:05:01.683 --> 00:05:04.766
log in them to our account
which is in the captcha state.

00:05:05.116 --> 00:05:07.733
We can send them
into the authentication flow

00:05:08.366 --> 00:05:11.233
and we make sure
that they come back to Facebook,

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:14.700
they don't consume the code
and the code is in the URL.

00:05:15.116 --> 00:05:17.866
Now the question is how to leak this code

00:05:17.966 --> 00:05:20.550
from this URL.

00:05:21.566 --> 00:05:25.200
Normally,
if you want to add a CAPTCHA functionality

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.416
from a third party
provider to your website,

00:05:27.566 --> 00:05:30.450
you have to import their scripts
on your website.

00:05:30.983 --> 00:05:35.000
It requires some level of trust
and to be even more secure,

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:40.850
Facebook didn't host this window, this iframe, on Facebook.com domain,

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:44.100
but on their sandbox domain.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:47.416
Sandbox
domains often improve security of websites

00:05:47.566 --> 00:05:51.050
because even though Facebook owns
these two domains,

00:05:51.616 --> 00:05:53.483
browsers don't know about it

00:05:53.483 --> 00:05:57.066
and they treat them like they would be
completely different ones.

00:05:57.083 --> 00:06:00.200
They have different cookies,
different storage, different everything.

00:06:00.533 --> 00:06:03.866
They just have one communication
channel to establish

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:05.933
if the captcha was filled in or not.

00:06:06.983 --> 00:06:09.016
But for the sandbox to be an improvement

00:06:09.016 --> 00:06:12.766
of security of the website,
it can't be used in too many contexts.

00:06:14.333 --> 00:06:17.866
But in this case it was
because it was also used

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:21.266
on the testing website
when the developers can see

00:06:21.616 --> 00:06:25.883
how a particular website
will look inside the Facebook mobile app.

00:06:26.666 --> 00:06:30.016
And this allowed them to upload
HTML files,

00:06:30.016 --> 00:06:32.966
which would be stored
on the same sandbox domain.

00:06:34.433 --> 00:06:36.166
And even though the sandbox

00:06:36.166 --> 00:06:40.483
domain is separate from Facebook.com,
the sandbox domains

00:06:40.616 --> 00:06:44.866
can somehow communicate with each other
because they are the same origin.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:49.016
And this is the last piece of our puzzle.

00:06:49.216 --> 00:06:52.816
Now let's combine them all together
to see the whole exploit.

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:56.566
Usually, in case of a client side attack,

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.933
you create your own website
and you have to trick the victim

00:06:59.933 --> 00:07:02.900
into visiting, for example, attacker.com
domain.

00:07:03.450 --> 00:07:06.833
In this case, we'll instead
host the whole exploit

00:07:06.983 --> 00:07:10.500
on the Facebook sandbox domain
because it will be helpful later.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:12.833
Before we attack

00:07:12.833 --> 00:07:16.400
the victim, we have to force our account
into the captcha state.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:20.033
Then, when the victim visits our exploit,

00:07:20.366 --> 00:07:24.100
first, we have to log them out
from their account using the CSRF.

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:29.166
Then, we have to log them into our account
using another CSRF.

00:07:29.166 --> 00:07:31.433
Then, we have this URL.

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:34.466
Importantly, we open this in a new tab and

00:07:34.466 --> 00:07:38.150
we save the reference to that window.

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:41.450
If the victim is already logged
in, in Gmail, Gmail

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:44.966
just flashes in front of them
and redirects back to Facebook.

00:07:45.466 --> 00:07:49.916
Facebook is in the captcha state,
which means they land on the captcha

00:07:49.916 --> 00:07:52.933
screen and importantly, the URL with

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:55.883
the code is inside the iframe's address.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:59.366
Now from the original window,

00:07:59.366 --> 00:08:02.183
we have the reference
to the newly opened window.

00:08:03.116 --> 00:08:07.550
These two are now in separate domains
because our website is on the sandbox

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:10.966
domain and the victim is now
on the Facebook.com domain.

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:15.766
So we only have limited access
to properties from Facebook.com.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:19.050
However, those limited

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:22.983
properties include the list of frames
on that website.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:27.083
And we know that the first iframe,
the ifame with the index zero,

00:08:27.416 --> 00:08:30.783
is the captcha window,
which is also on the sandbox domain.

00:08:32.366 --> 00:08:35.183
And all of a sudden, our exploit website

00:08:35.783 --> 00:08:39.383
and the iframe
with the sensitive code in the address

00:08:40.066 --> 00:08:43.516
are on the same origin, meaning
they can communicate with each other.

00:08:43.966 --> 00:08:46.816
And our exploit website has access

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:50.533
to the address of the iframe,
which contains the code.

00:08:51.566 --> 00:08:53.750
We now send the code to our server

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:56.816
and we need a script to exchange this code

00:08:57.016 --> 00:09:00.533
for a victim's session cookie,
taking over their account.

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:03.983
So the bug allowed to take over
any Facebook

00:09:03.983 --> 00:09:06.900
account
of someone that uses Gmail to log in.

00:09:07.216 --> 00:09:10.400
The reward for it was almost $45,000.

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:13.466
Of course, the bug is already fixed.

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:15.266
Check out this

00:09:15.266 --> 00:09:18.500
playlist to see two more account
takeovers on Facebook

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:22.616
that were rewarded $25k and $55k.

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:26.700
And if you enjoyed this video
leave a like and subscribe to the channel.

00:09:27.016 --> 00:09:29.600
For now,
thank you for watching and goodbye!

